UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 22, Lesson 1: Collage and layering', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small details on top. Tear paper for soft edges and cut it for crisp edges. Plan your layout before gluing and use a thin layer of glue. Pieces that overlap look closer to the viewer, which helps make the picture feel three-dimensional.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a collage of a night sky over a town. Say what goes in layer 1, layer 2 and layer 3, and which edges you would tear or cut.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper moon. Cut for crisp houses and tear for the soft moon.'' Overlapping creates depth.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect magazine pages, wrapping paper or coloured scraps. Plan a three-layer collage of a landscape, move the pieces before gluing, then glue. Label which pieces are torn and which are cut and say how overlapping helped the depth.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small details on top. Tear paper for soft edges and cut it for crisp edges. Plan your layout before gluing and use a thin layer of glue. Pieces that overlap look closer to the viewer, which helps make the picture feel three-dimensional.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Collect magazine pages, wrapping paper or coloured scraps. Plan a three-layer collage of a landscape, move the pieces before gluing, then glue. Label which pieces are torn and which are cut and say how overlapping helped Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper moon. Cut for crisp houses and tear for… Reasoning: I used a collage by attending to layering rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Correction: Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a collage.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a collage on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a collage, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper… This matches the lesson skill for a collage.","options":["Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper…","Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Plan a collage of a night sky over a town. Say what goes in layer 1, layer 2 and layer 3, and which edges you would tear or cut. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so…","Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper…","Careful use of layering with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named layering, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the layering used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a collage. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a collage.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about layering?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Ella''s collage of a pond. Layer 1: blue paper background. Layer 2: green torn paper lily pads, some overlapping. Layer 3: a small…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface.… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down.","Collect magazine pages, wrapping paper or coloured scraps. Plan a three-layer collage of a landscape, move the pieces before gluing, then glue.…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a collage on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper moon. Cut for crisp houses and tear for… Reasoning: I used a collage by attending to layering rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a collage, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Correction: Strong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a collage.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w22-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a collage.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a collage with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify layering and explain why it matters.","I can practise overlapping independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a background needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small…","options":["A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small details on top. Tear paper for soft edges and cut it for crisp edges. Plan your layout before gluing and use a thin layer of glue. Pieces that overlap look closer to the viewer, which helps make the picture feel three-dimensional.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ella''s collage of a pond. Layer 1: blue paper background. Layer 2: green torn paper lily pads, some overlapping. Layer 3: a small cut-out yellow frog sitting on top of one pad. The frog looks closest because it is on top, and the torn edges of the pads look soft like leaves. She plans the layout by moving the pieces before gluing.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a collage of a night sky over a town. Say what goes in layer 1, layer 2 and layer 3, and which edges you would tear or cut.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper moon. Cut for crisp houses and tear for the soft moon.'' Overlapping creates depth.","explain":"A collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small details on top. Tear paper for soft edges and cut it for crisp edges. Plan your layout before gluing and use a thin layer of glue. Pieces that overlap look closer to the viewer, which helps make the picture feel three-dimensional.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Collage layers.","caption":"Background to detail.","svg