mment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ella''s collage of a pond. Layer 1: blue paper background. Layer 2: green torn paper lily pads, some overlapping. Layer 3: a small cut-out yellow frog sitting on top of one pad. The frog looks closest because it is on top, and the torn edges of the pads look soft like leaves. She plans the layout by moving the pieces before gluing. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Torn and cut edges.","caption":"Tear or cut on purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Torn edge</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">soft, like leaves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cut edge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">crisp, like buildings</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ella''s collage of a pond. Layer 1: blue paper background. Layer 2: green torn paper lily pads, some overlapping. Layer 3: a small cut-out yellow frog sitting on top of one pad. The frog looks closest because it is on top, and the torn edges of the pads look soft like leaves. She plans the layout by moving the pieces before gluing.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Layer 1: dark blue background. Layer 2: black cut-out houses along the bottom. Layer 3: small yellow cut-out windows and a torn white paper moon. Cut for crisp houses and tear for the soft moon.'' Overlapping creates depth.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that covering all the paper with glue will make pieces stick better. Too much glue wrinkles and soaks the paper, so use a thin layer and press down. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a collage?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA collage is an artwork made by gluing pieces of paper, fabric or other flat materials onto a surface. Layering means placing pieces on top of each other, so overlapping shapes create depth. Start with a background layer, then add bigger shapes in the middle layer and small details on top. Tear paper for soft edges and cut it for crisp edges. Plan your layout before gluing and use a thin layer of glue. Pieces that overlap look closer to the viewer, which helps make the picture feel three-dimensional. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 22, Lesson 1: Collage and layering","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Printmaking, collage and texture","week":22,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ella''s collage of a pond. Layer 1: blue paper background. Layer 2: green torn paper lily pads, some overlapping. Layer 3: a small cut-out yellow frog sitting on top of one pad. The frog looks closest because it is on top, and the torn edges of the pads look soft like leaves. She plans the layout by moving the pieces before gluing.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Collage layers.","caption":"Background to detail.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Layer 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">blue background</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Layer 3</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small frog on top</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Torn and cut edges.","caption":"Tear or cut on purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Torn edge</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">soft, like leaves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cut edge</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">crisp, like buildings</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w22-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
